    "chunk_size_bytes": 1565,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cMannar is a prime example because ,beneath its sands lie vast reserves of ilmenite, rutile and zircon, minerals coveted by global industries for titanium, aerospace and ceramics. Companies such as Australia\u2019s Titanium Sands Limited have aggressively pursued extraction projects while local environmental scientists warn that mining in Mannar could deplete freshwater aquifers worsening chronic water scarcity, destroy sea grass beds and coral reefs undermining fisheries, vital to 15,000 families and accelerate coastal erosion, increasing vulnerability to climate disasters.\n\nOn account of these large scale wind and sand mining projects, since early 2024, Mannar has witnessed some of the largest environmental protests in Sri Lanka\u2019s recent history, uniting communities, faith leaders and civil society in unprecedented fashion. In October this year a fundamental rights petition was filed in the Supreme Court challenging the proposed heavy mineral sand extraction project on Mannar Island seeking an order suspending the operation of the Terms of Reference (TOR) and the licence issued for mining activities in the Mannar area.\n\nThe next court date is in February 2026 perhaps that is why the Australian side is now redoubling its support to the Australian mining companies! But will failed public diplomacy result in slowing down the protests or will it spur them on? Journalists should be aware of the complexity of the issues in Mannar and reflect all sides instead of just reporting on seminars and press briefings by one plainly well-endowed party."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-01-03-islandlk-33129040",
