    "chunk_size_bytes": 1630,
    "chunk_text": "One way the press can help is by exposing the underlying conditions of multilateral and bilateral agreements. In any analysis of the different flows of foreign assistance into Sri Lanka, there is a need to highlight the direct cash transfers which support the Treasury and are the most valuable for the government. Remittances received from expatriates fall into this category and the newspapers carry countless stories of our local heroes who have been in the forefront of raising assistance for Sri Lanka. A number of countries even small neighbours like Maldives, Nepal and Bhutan have also provided direct donations to the Sri Lanka Government.\n\nOf late, local television has been presenting short films on television of the good results shown through the transfer of technology from China and Japan as implemented and described by local counterparts in areas such as water purification and hillside stabilization. The financial conditions of such transfer of technology are not mentioned, but it is so much better public diplomacy.\n\n(Sarala Fernando retired from the Foreign Ministry as Additional Secretary; her last ambassadorial appointment was as Permanent Representative to the UN and International Organizations in Geneva . Her Ph.D was on India-Sri Lanka relations and she works now on foreign policy, public diplomacy and protection of heritage).\n\nA question of \u201cculling\u201d surplus cattle in govt. farms & handling appeals\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
